\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends at record low despite support. \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2000\par 12:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, June 2 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended at new closing low on Friday amid dollar demand from corporates, but dollar sales by state-run banks and exporters helped the rupee to recover from intra-day lows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at 44.625/635 off mid-morning lows of 44.65 per dollar. It had opened steady 44.5625/5725 compared to its previous close of 44.56/57. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said state-run banks which had protected the rupee from slipping past the 44.60 mark on Thursday, reportedly sold dollars at 44.65 level. \par \par \uc2  These state-run banks are considered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's proxy and often sells dollars to keep the rupee stable. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the bank had quoted along with the market in morning trade before the Indian rupee hit 44.65. \par \par \uc2  Demand for dollars on Friday was mainly corporate driven, they said. Earlier in the day, large corporates, including a large petrochemical firm bought dollars triggering the slide in the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to defend the 44.65 level now," a dealer in a private sector bank said. \par \par \uc2  Last week the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally when the rupee fell to its weakest level of 44.75, and warned against speculation and imposed fresh measures to contain volatility. \par \par \uc2  It also said it will sell dollars through the State Bank of India to meet \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs for oil imports and some government debt payments. \par \par \uc2  Forwards premia rose despite easy call money rates on reporting day amid paying pressure from a large state-run bank after the spot rupee weakened. \par \par \uc2  The six monthly forward premia annualised was at 2.62 percent compared to the previous close of 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000, fax +91 22 264-1699, anirban.nag@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6204a8e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
