\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee dips slightly after steady open. \b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2000\par 05:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, June 2 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee opened steady on Friday and but slipped in early trade on corporate demand for dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at 44.5625/5725 per dollar and was quoted at 44.59/60 at 9:20 a.m (0350 GMT). It closed at 44.56/57 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Some corporate demand has some in. The State Bank of India (SBI) is quoting along with the market and this has led to the dip in the rupee," a dealer in a private sector bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said inter-bank demand to cover short positions had still not emerged. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected SBI to step in to prevent the rupee from weakening beyond 44.60. \par \par \uc2  The largest commercial bank in India is considered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's proxy and often sells dollars to keep the rupee stable. \par \par \uc2  "There will be some demand, but one expects the Reserve Bank of India to protect a critical level," a dealer in a large foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  State-run banks led by the SBI had stepped in and sold dollars on Thursday after the rupee hit 44.62 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said current levels could trigger export sales. \par \par \uc2  Exporters had sold dollars on Thursday after the rupee crossed the 44.60 mark. \par \par \uc2  Last week the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the rupee fell to its weakest level of 44.75, warning the market against speculation and imposing some measures to contain volatility. \par \par \uc2  It also said that it will sell dollars through the SBI to meet \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs towards oil imports and some government debt payments. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000, fax +91 22 264-1699, anirban.nag@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw62046so\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
