\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends at new low, SBI checks slide. \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2000\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 30 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended at a record low on Tuesday amid sustained dollar demand from importers during the day, but its slide was checked by the State Bank of India's (SBI) dollar supplies, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended at 44.565/575 per dollar, its lowest close. \par \par \uc2  It has been ending at new lows in the past three days after last week's dip to an all-time low in intra-day trade and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's measures stirred a lot of importers out of complacency. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened Tuesday at 44.49/51 before sliding on heavy dollar buying by importers, including a state-run oil firm's $40-50 million requirement, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The SBI was there protecting the 44.58 level...except for a brief minute when the SBI was off the market, dollar demand was taken care of," a dealer with a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the SBI's continuous presence indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably did not want the rupee to weaken beyond current levels. \par \par \uc2  "Now the exporters who are holding back sales will slowly emerge," a dealer with a state-run bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the rupee hit lows of 44.75 last week with a warning against speculation. \par \par \uc2  The RBI also imposed a 50 percent surcharge on the import finance rate and raised the interest on overdue export bills to 25 percent. \par \par \uc2  It said it will continue to sell dollars through the State Bank of India (SBI) and will meet \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs towards oil imports and some government debt payments. \par \par \uc2  The RBI said it was not considering monetary tightening immediately. But on Tuesday it placed on its open market sale list the 10.95 percent 2011 bond auctioned on Monday, which devolved heavily on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It also placed several 364-day treasury bills on its sale list. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said forward premia however moved lower amid receiving across most maturities. \par \par \uc2  "Call rates are easy, the bond auction did not take any liquidity out of the market and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has assured liquidity will not be tightened...why should the market be paying," the chief dealer at a private bank said. \par \par \uc2  The six-month premium ended at an annualised 2.63 percent compared with Monday's 2.79. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay Newsroom +91-22-265 9000; fax 91-22-264 1708; vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw5u04hrv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
