\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee weak but steady in afternoon deals. \b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2000\par 11:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 29 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee traded in a weaker range in Monday afternoon deals amid persistent light dollar purchases by banks and corporates. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said import demand was a trickle, yet kept the rupee from firming. \par \par \uc2  Purchases by foreign funds through custodian banks was also adding to the dollar demand, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee was quoted at 44.47/48 per dollar at 2:35 p.m. (0905 GMT), having slipped from opening levels of 44.36/39. \par \par \uc2  "There is underlying import demand, but no big corporates are there in the market today," said a dealer with a private bank. \par \par \uc2  A gradual waning of import demand, which was heavy last week and caused the rupee's slide to all-time lows of 44.75, indicated the rupee will soon settle into a range, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Exporters waiting for the rupee to stabilise to sell dollars at peak levels may soon show interest, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's slide last week forced the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a warning against speculation. \par \par \uc2  It also imposed a 50 percent surcharge on the import finance rate and raised the interest on overdue export bills to 25 percent to speed dollar inflows and prevent bunching up of demand. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will continue to sell dollars through the State Bank of India (SBI) and will meet \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs towards oil imports and some government debt payments. \par \par \uc2  Forward premia in far maturities were stable in afternoon deals but there was receiving interest (buy-sell swaps) in the near-term following the dip in call rates, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The one-month premium was down at an annualised 2.38 percent compared with highs of 2.52 and Friday's 3.05 percent. \par \par \uc2  Call rates closed at 6.9-7.1 percent, down from opening levels around 7.25-7.5 percent, with liquidity boosted by bond redemptions and a view Monday's 11-year bond auction will receive poor subscription leading to lower outlfows. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay Newsroom +91-22-265 9000; fax 91-22-264 1708; vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw5t04oa3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
