\par\par \b \uc2  Indian call open above 8 pct, low supplies seen. \b0\par\par\uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2000\par 06:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 24 (Reuters) - Indian call money opened above eight percent on Thursday on lower supplies and tight short-term liquidity conditions in the money market. \par \par \uc2  Call money opened at 8.10-8.25 percent compared to the previous close of 8.0-8.10 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said lenders were quoting higher levels after outflows towards a "on-tap" bond sale and open market sales of treasury bills. \par \par \uc2  "With the rupee weakening gradually, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep short-term liquidity tight and curb volatility," a primary dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed outflows worth 3 billion rupees towards the treasury bills sales on May 24. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) announced a sale list of six 364-day treasury bills through open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee extended its losses in Thursday morning trade and was quoted at 44.18/20 at 9:35 a.m (0405 GMT) leading money market traders to believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step in and stem the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee closed at an all time low of 44.145/155 per dollar on Wednesday on persistent dollar buying by firms and short covering by banks. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest commercial bank, the State Bank of India (SBI) and other state-run banks by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said rupee supplies would get sucked out of the market if state-run banks, who are traditional lenders in the money market, sold dollars to prop up the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected an auction to coincide with inflows worth 36 billion rupees on Monday next would keep call rates above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refinance rates for the next few days. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000, fax +91 22 264-1699, anirban.nag@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw5p04zhm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
