\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee weakens further on early dlr demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2000\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 15 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee weakened further and touched 44.05 to the dollar after opening soft on Monday amid persistent inter-bank demand for dollars in volatile early trade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The absence of any moves by the State Bank of India (SBI) to sell dollars added to the rupee's weakness, they said. \par \par \uc2  India's largest commercial bank, the SBI, often sets the trend in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and it's moves are keenly watched by dealers. \par \par \uc2  The rupee recovered marginally to 43.99/44.01 at 9:43 (0414 GMT), off lows of 44.03/05 at 9:34 a.m (0404 GMT). \par \par \uc2  The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at 43.93/95 against the dollar compared with Friday's close of 43.92/93. \par \par \uc2  "The demand for dollars on Friday has continued in early trade on Monday. The rupee should remain soft against the dollar during the day," a dealer at a large foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the rupee slid from highs of 43.85 driven mostly by inter-bank demand as corporates stayed on the sidelines, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "However some large corporates were also making dollar bids on Monday," a dealer in a state-run bank said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's unexpected 0.85 percent fall to all-time lows of 44.08 on Wednesday, and partial recovery on Thursday, were both driven by the SBI, they said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both days, but dealers believe the SBI was acting on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said forward premiums were likely to be ranged as the market awaits the result of the twin bond auction later in the day and its impact on liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India will conduct a twin bond auction of 2003 and 2006 maturity government bonds worth 50 billion rupees later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The six-month premium ended at an annualised 2.42 percent on Friday against 2.47 percent the previous day. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000, fax +91 22 264-1699, anirban.nag@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5f00m9s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
