\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Indian rupee recovers as market weighs next move. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2000\par 09:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 11 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee settled into a wide and slightly firmer range on Thursday morning as the market waited for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to signal a direction a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to its lowest levels ever. \par \par \uc2  Traders said sentiment was still a little bearish, and the market would be jittery until the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) either indicated its intention or stayed away from the market for a reasonable period. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SBI) and other state-run banks, and the market believes Wednesday's sharp fall was a move engineered by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "All eyes are on the RBI. Sentiment is still slightly negative for the rupee," said V. Ravikumar, chief dealer at ABN Amro Bank. \par \par \uc2  The rupee fell 0.85 percent to a record low of 44.08 per dollar on Wednesday, largely because SBI, India's largest commercial bank, bought dollars continuously. \par \par \uc2  It was quoted in a wide range of 43.94-43.98 in noon trade, firmer than the opening 44.03/06 per dollar on Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  Trade was quiet, partly on account of a nationwide strike by bank employees to protest against privatisation which dampened cash market transactions. \par \par \uc2  ADJUSTMENT FOR STRONG DOLLAR, INFLATION \par \par \uc2  The market speculated the RBI may have wanted a weaker rupee for several reasons, including the need to adjust the real effective exchange rate and keep it on par with other Asian and global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have been falling. \par \par \uc2  "...it's just a one percent adjustment after a year of stability, it's quite tiny. If you look at it as a weakening against a very strong dollar, it's really an adjustment for dollar strength rather than rupee weakness," Sanjeev Sanyal, regional economist at Deutsche bank told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  India has strict capital controls and curbs on speculation, and the rupee has lost just three percent in 12 months, inclusive of Wednesday's sharp slide. \par \par \uc2  In the past few months, major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Japanese yen and the euro, as well as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah, have weakened. \par \par \uc2  The baht has lost 3.7 percent and the rupiah 15 percent since the end of the previous year. \par \par \uc2  Local inflation is rising, with wholesale prices at a 70-week high of 6.1 percent, but core inflation is still low. \par \par \uc2  "They (the RBI) are likely to keep it here, watch what happens on inflation, the dollar and then, should anything move on those fronts, they will adjust again. This is going to be small discreet moves," said Sanyal. \par \par \uc2  WILL MARKETS TAKE OVER? \par \par \uc2  By noon on Thursday, the market was veering to the view the planned weakening for the rupee had ended and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts would be to ensure the new band is maintained. \par \par \uc2  The rupee, technically a gradually depreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has been very strong in the past year amid large foreign investment. This has boosted confidence in it, with most importers choosing not to hedge payables. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said Wednesday's move had unsettled some corporates, but if they perceived the rupee would stabilise soon, the current demand for dollars would subside. \par \par \uc2  Flows into India have been positive so far. Portfolio flows have crossed $1.6 billion until now, compared with just $1.5 billion in all of 1999. Direct investment flows could also be strong with corporates having lined up overseas issues, provided the technology sector's current slump does not last. \par \par \uc2  Flows could dwindle if the United States hikes rates to counter inflation and foreign investors cut down on Asian assets. ((Bombay Newsroom +91-22-265 9000; fax 91-22-264 1708; vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5b00pvy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
