\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Ends At Record Low INR44.04/Dlr (INR43.6775) \b0\par\par\uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2000\par 12:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- Dollar-buying by companies, driven by the absence of support from the Reserve Bank of India pushed the Indian rupee to a record closing low against the dollar Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended Wednesday at 44.04 rupees, up sharply from the INR43.6775 at Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  The previous record low hit in August 1998 was INR43.6950. \par \par \uc2  The absence of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as aggressive indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hurt sentiment on the rupee, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  State Bank of India, seen as the Reserve Bank's proxy in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, bought dollars in early trade and that set the trend for the rest of the session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened steady, but began its ascent when State Bank bought dollars at INR43.6950, the highest bid in the market at that time. This pushed the dollar to INR43.75. At this level State Bank sold a token amount of dollars to support the Indian unit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar however continued to rise on dollar-buying by State Bank of India and other banks. \par \par \uc2  The weakening rupee also attracted a lot of companies, who covered their near-term positions and bought aggressively. \par \par \uc2  A few large Indian companies also bought dollars, when the rupee continued to weaken. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose all the way to INR44.08, the rupee's record traded low, by the end of the session. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  however, appeared unperturbed by the developments and didn't lend any support to the Indian unit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market Wednesday. Although State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , perceived to be its surrogate, did step in to protect the Indian rupee. It sold merely a token amount and then continued to buy dollars on behalf of its clients. \par \par \uc2  "There was no direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ineffective," a dealer at a foreign bank pointed out. \par \par \uc2  This has led the market to believe that the fall wasn't just with its tacit approval, but was probably engineered by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is expected to rise further Thursday, unless State Bank sells dollars to protect the Indian unit. \par \par \uc2  Rupee premiums on the forward dollar ended higher on corporate dollar-buying in the forward market. \par \par \uc2  The premium on six-month dollars ended at INR0.46 Wednesday up from INR0.44 Tuesday. One-year premiums closed at INR1.14 Wednesday, up from INR1.06 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Ramya Venugopal; 91-22-288-4211; \par \par \uc2  ramya.venugopal@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5a02bv7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
