\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Down Early; State Bank Isn't Selling Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 214 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2000\par 06:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- The Indian rupee is lower in early trade Wednesday as banks continue to buy dollars after State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0410 GMT, the dollar was at 43.6850 rupees, up from its previous close of INR43.6775. The dollar opened Wednesday at INR43.6725. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, the rupee ended at its lowest-ever closing level on dollar demand from companies and foreign institutional investors. \par \par \uc2  "The market was expecting State Bank to sell (dollars). But it is remaining neutral and was quoting the dollar INR43.68-INR43.69," a dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the dollar to trade between INR43.67 and INR43.70 Wednesday. "But it will be difficult for the dollar to breach INR43.70," a dealer at a private bank said. \par \par \uc2  State Bank of India, the country's largest commercial bank, usually buys and sells dollars for state-owned companies and also for the Reserve Bank of India, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not want to artificially support the rupee if it sees genuine demand from customers. \par \par \uc2  -By S. Anuradha; 9122-2884211; su.anuradha@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5a02b81\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
