\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Ends Flat; Dlr Demand Offsets Sales \b0\par\par\uc2 322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2000\par 13:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- The Indian rupee ended steady against the dollar Tuesday as dollars sales in the market were offset by dollar-buying by importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 43.6550 rupees, little changed from Monday's close of INR43.6525. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded in a narrow range between INR43.6525 and INR43.6575 during the session. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was considerable demand for dollars from companies that were buying ahead of the month-end. There were also importers who were buying for their immediate requirements, they added. \par \par \uc2  While State Bank of India was holding its dollar quote at INR43.6525-INR43.6575, there were dollar sales by other foreign banks as well. \par \par \uc2  State Bank of India is the country's largest commercial bank and is seen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's proxy in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is often believed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  through State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was banks were reluctant to take positions ahead of the Reserve Bank's monetary and credit policy announcement Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, bankers said there was a tendency towards a higher dollar on corporate dollar demand, but added that further weakening of the rupee will take place only if the Reserve Bank permits it. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank has been keeping a tight hold on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the past twenty months after the Indian unit fell to its all-time low of 43.6950 against the dollar in August 1998. \par \par \uc2  In the forward market, the premium on six-month forward dollars ended at INR0.62, or an annualized 2.84%, compared with INR0.6250, or an annualized 2.87%. \par \par \uc2  One-year premiums ended INR1.3750, or 3.15% annualized, against INR1.3650 or 3.12% annualized. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Ramya Venugopal; 91-22-288-4211; ramya.venugopal@dowjones.com\par -0- 25/04/00 11-32G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4p022kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
