\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee opens steady, seen in weaker range. \b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2000\par 06:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOMBAY, April 17 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee\tab     opened Monday little-changed from its previous close against the dollar and dealers expected it to come under pressure after the early crash in Asian stockmarkets on the tails of Friday's sharp fall in U.S. markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupee was quoted at 43.645/6525 per dollar against the previous close of 43.645/65.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "The open is definitely steady. But with the stockmarket crash, the dollar will be well bid today," a dealer at an American bank said. \par \par \uc2  "The (dollar) shorts will get covered, people will look to remain long and those with dollar supplies will prefer to wait and watch." \par \par \uc2  Asian stockmarkets fell sharply and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were weak on Monday tracking the fall in the technology-laden Nasdaq index and the blue-chip Dow Jones index on Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The technology-laden Nasdaq index\tab    plunged 355.49 points or 9.67 percent on Friday at 3,321.29 - a level not seen since November.\par The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average\tab   lost a record 617.78 points, or 5.66 percent, to end the week at 10,305.77 amid concerns of rising inflation in the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said with the rupee just a whisker away from its all-time low of 43.70, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was likely to ensure it did not fall too sharply by clamping down on any market volatility.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In the the normal course they may have let it meandered down. But today, I don't think, they will let it slip much," a dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (RBI) rarely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and mostly does it through a clutch of state-run banks, mainly the country's largest bank the State Bank of India , dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They said SBI offered dollars at 43.65 levels in early trading, but were unsure if it was on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's behalf.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said dollar sales could be expected during the day as banks offload dollar stocks accumulated over the long weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Banks and financial markets in most centres were closed on Friday and Saturday for festival holidays.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Actions of custodian banks of foreign funds will be closely watched, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Forward dollar premia were expected to be higher and dealers said a breach of 43.66 levels could see corporates hedging their near term exposures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The six-month premium ended Thursday at an annualised 2.73 percent against the previous close of 2.73 percent. ((Bombay Newsroom +91-22-265 9000; fax 91-22-264 1708; santosh.menon@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4h023z9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
