\par\par \b \uc2  India Defense Spending Hike Absolutely Unavoidable-Sinha \b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 05:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI -(Dow Jones)- Indian Finance Minister Yashwant Sinha said Wednesday that his proposal for increased defense spending in the budget for the financial year 2000-01 was "absolutely unavoidable." \par \par \uc2  Sinha proposed to increase defense spending by 130 billion rupees ($1=INR43.54), 20% higher than the revised figures and 28% higher than the original estimate for the coming year. This is the largest ever increase in the defense budget in a single year. \par \par \uc2  He also said that he expects to exceed the INR100 billion targeted level of receipts the government hopes to get from the sale of its shares in state-owned companies. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sinha also reiterated that the government had the "strongest\par determination," to proceed with its privatization target. He was\par speaking to industrialists Wednesday about his budget proposals\par presented to Parliament Tuesday.\par - - 01/03/00 05-42G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sinha also said that he had created the right conditions for an\par interest rate cut, which was the best he could do, given that the actual\par signal should come from the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I have stubbornly refused over the past two years to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with\par the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " in its decisions on interest rates, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "My responsibility is to create the necessary conditions" for interest\par rate cuts, he said. "So what have I done. I have reduced interest rates\par on General Provident Fund by one percentage point. Those who criticize\par me for not doing enough to reduce the interest burden should take note\par of this fact," Sinha added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government reduced the interest rate on the General Provident Fund\par for government employees to 11% from 12% as part of the budget\par proposals.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Sinha said the fall of the rupee against the\par dollar over the past year has benefitted exporters significantly. When\par combined with no tax on export earnings, it represents too much\par favoritism for the export sector.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupee has fallen 2.5% from INR42.51 over the past 12 months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I hope you'll agree with me that there is no reason anymore to treat\par the export sector differently," Sinha said. "Export income has to be\par taxed."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I have tried to do my best...we have taken the reform process very\par strongly forward in this budget," Sinha said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Denny Kurien; Denny.Kurien@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sinha also said the time was not yet ripe for full capital account\par convertibility. The rupee is currently partly convertible on the current\par account, but not for capital account transactions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The time has not come for full convertibility," Sinha told\par industrialists.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "As for corporates, the RBI is working on it and will come out in due\par course with rules for capital account convertibility for the\par corporates," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The exchange rate is market-determined and the only thing the Reserve\par Bank of India can do is to watch the market and check any undue\par fluctuations, Sinha said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He declined to comment on the rupee-dollar level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sinha's Chief Economic Adviser Shankar N. Acharya said in a\par post-budget press conference Wednesday that the government has assumed\par an "average" rupee-dollar level at 45 rupees. The Indian rupee was\par trading at INR43.61 against the dollar in late morning trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We have taken it at 45 rupees at the average" for computing what the\par total import bill will be, Acharya said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is stable now because of the confidence of\par investors in the Indian economy. If there is any volatility in the\par market, the RBI will step in to curb it, Sinha said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The minister also said he wasn't overly concerned about the fall in\par India's stock markets following his budget speech, and expected the\par market to "bounce back" once players get a chance to examine his tax and\par spending proposals in more detail.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bombay Stock Exchange Sensitive Index, or Sensex, fell 5.1%\par Tuesday after the budget revealed higher taxes on dividends paid by\par companies, some taxes on the export earnings from the services sector\par and raised excise duties on a range of products.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, the Sensex was up 1.76% midday Wednesday to 5542.68 points\par from Tuesday's close.\par -0- 01/03/00 06-37G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw31012n1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
