\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Indian rupee at 13-mth low despite cbank. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 1999\par 14:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Sept 29 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell slightly on technical factors on Wednesday, but enough to notch a 13-month low despite some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support. \par \par \uc2  Underlying dollar demand from importers and technical factors caused the fall, and the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to curb the fall. \par \par \uc2  The weakness was anticipated and largely due to month-end factors, but the latest decline brought the rupee within striking range of a 43.70 all-time low hit in August 1998. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has been showing signs of weakness and in the past two weeks it has lost 10 paise (0.1 rupee). It ended at a record closing low of 43.59/595 per dollar on Wednesday after hitting an intra-day low of 43.61. \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of underlying dollar demand and 43.60 is a psychological level," said Ravi Pai, treasury vice-president at HDFC Bank. \par \par \uc2  The chief dealer at a foreign bank said: "Although the state-run banks supplied dollars in the afternoon, the way it was lapped up by the market and the rupee still ended weak shows how much more of dollar demand there is." \par \par \uc2  A market holiday on September 30 for banks' half-yearly closure of accounts led to large demand for dollars from banks and corporates reaping benefits of high swap differences over the month-end. \par \par \uc2  ANXIETY OVER RBI SUPPORT \par \par \uc2  Bankers say corporate import remittances are picking up because of fears the RBI will relax its hold on the rupee once elections are over. \par \par \uc2  The last stage of India's five-phase election is to be held this weekend and results are due to be announced on October 6. Exit polls project a victory for the alliance the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) is leading. \par \par \uc2  The RBI had in August announced it would bridge any dollar supply gaps in the market through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep most of the country's oil imports and defence payments out of the market. \par \par \uc2  That safeguard for the rupee is expected to remain in place until a new government is formed. \par \par \uc2  Foreign institutional investors (FIIs) have shied away from Indian markets in the past two months, but there is hope inflows will pick up once the elections give the BJP a clear mandate. \par \par \uc2  "After the elections, we could even see upward pressure on the rupee if FII inflows pick up," said Pai. \par \par \uc2  FIIs have sold close to $200 million in Indian equity and debt markets since August after being net buyers of close to $1 billion in the April-July period. \par \par \uc2  Besides shrinking foreign investment, analysts point to a host of factors the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has to contend with soon. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil prices, still close to $24 per barrel, are a cause for worry, and gold prices at 16-month peaks above $300 are also expected to hurt the trade balance. \par \par \uc2  The country's oil imports are expected to cross the $10 billion mark in 1999/2000 (April-March) from close to $6 billion last year. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9t02ytz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
