\par\par \b \uc2  RBI defence keeps Indian rupee firm, premiums fall. \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 1999\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 23 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee was traded in a firm range in mid-morning deals on Monday amid good two-way trading and forward premiums were down after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would bridge any demand-supply gaps to defend the rupee, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee was quoted at 43.50/51 per dollar at 10:30 a.m (0500 GMT), little changed from its opening levels of 43.49/52, but off Friday's all-time closing low of 43.565/57. \par \par \uc2  Minutes before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market opened for trading, the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) said it was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly or sell dollars through the State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SBI)  in order to augment dollar supply in the market. \par \par \uc2  The RBI said it was also prepared to meet the country's debt service obligations directly. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar bids had emerged after some early sales of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which took the rupee to 43.49. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee tried to firm past 43.50 levels, but there appears to be some corporate dollar demand. Probably corporates which could not buy dollars last week are buying now," the chief dealer at an American bank said. \par \par \uc2  But there were no dollar sales by the RBI or the SBI, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "So far the talking up the rupee has not brought dollar sellers out in a big way. Banks are sitting short dollars after the morning's statements and will possibly look to cover that before the end of the day," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said forward premiums opened sharply lower on the back of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statements and easier call money rates. \par \par \uc2  "Premiums opened with a gap today. Exporters are booking profits after the RBI statements," a private bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Probably the bigger thing is that call money rates are down." \par \par \uc2  Call money was traded between 8.50-8.80 percent in mid-morning trade on Monday after hovering around 10 percent most of last week. \par \par \uc2  The six-month premium was quoted at an annualised 4.83 percent against the previous close of five percent. ((Santosh Menon, Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, santosh.menon@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8n0216c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
