\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee slips on demand, SBI relaxes grip. \b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1999\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 10 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee extended losses in late-morning trade on Tuesday on corporate demand for dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee was traded at 43.45 per dollar, weakening from opening levels of 43.38/40 and Monday's close of 43.395/405. \par \par \uc2  At 11:30 a.m (0600 GMT), the rupee was quoted at 43.435/445 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said importer demand for dollars continued unabated. A large term lender also appeared interested in buying dollars, they added. \par \par \uc2  "Corporates are buying. But it's in small lots...It (demand) is not coming from any particular quarter," a foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the country's largest bank, the State Bank of India , which rescued the rupee from a sharp slide on Monday, relaxed its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Through the morning he (SBI) kept quoting 43.38/40. But he had started raising the offer," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "43.40 was a artificial level. It was okay yesterday (Monday) as it shook off the longs in the market. But there seems to be good demand at that level today, some of which is coming from other state-run banks." \par \par \uc2  SBI, which is often considered a conduit for the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold dollars on Monday at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behest helping the rupee recover from lows of 43.585, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the 43.45 level to hold for some time. \par \par \uc2  "There seem to be supplies at 43.45 levels. I think that should be the barrier for the time being at least," a broker said. \par \par \uc2  Forward premiums were up tracking the weaker spot rupee, but dealers said they expected SBI to receive (buy-sell swaps) premiums. \par \par \uc2  The six-month premium was quoted at an annualised 4.81 percent against the previous close of 4.71 percent. ((Santosh Menon, Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8a02wkw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
