\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  halts rupee's slide. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 1999\par 16:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 9 (Reuters) - Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought the rupee's accelerating slide to a juddering halt on Monday, but analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set for further falls. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said leading public sector banks, probably at the behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold dollars prompting a sudden recovery in the rupee as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  headed toward all time lows set a year ago. \par \par \uc2  While the rupee has stabilised for now, most analysts still foresee the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gradually weakening. \par \par \uc2  Several factors are conspiring against the rupee's stability. \par \par \uc2  Global oil prices have surged, Indian gold imports have ballooned and an expected hike in U.S interest rates could reduce investment inflows from foreign funds. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the rupee was fast nearing its all-time low of 43.70, struck last August, when dollar selling by the country's largest commercial bank, State Bank of India (SBI), stopped the rot at 43.585. \par \par \uc2  It closed at 43.395/405 per dollar, firmer than Friday's close of 43.47/475. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupee's sudden slump from around 43.30 a week ago had gathered pace due to large demand from state-run firms and oil importers. \par \par \uc2  Bankers and corporates who had predicted the rupee would slip to 44 or 45 per dollar by March 2000, have not revised forecasts and some believe the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) will be content to let the market weaken the rupee slightly. \par \par \uc2  The rupee, only partially convertible, is protected by RBI curbs on speculation although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stresses the market can set the rupee's trend so long as it does not move too fast. \par \par \uc2  "They (the RBI) will leave it to the market to determine what should be the right level for the rupee. That is what they have been saying all the while. What they are worried about is the volatile swings in the exchange rate," S.Anand, Senior Vice-President, IDBI Bank told Reuters Television on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Economic indicators have turned favourable in recent months, exports have recovered and the approaching mid-term elections have largely been discounted. \par \par \uc2  India reported 6.5 percent growth in exports in the first quarter of the year (April-March). But a 53 percent rise in oil imports, driven by global oil prices surging to 20-month highs over $20 per barrel, has cast a cloud. \par \par \uc2  "If international oil prices remain over $20 a barrel for another two to three months, it will be a real cause for concern," Chetan Ahya, economist at BNP Prime Peregrine India said. \par \par \uc2  Gold, India's second biggest import item may be out of favour in world markets but the cheap price has only fuelled appetite here, according to local newspaper reports. \par \par \uc2  The Business Standard daily said there was a 32 percent rise in gold imports in the first quarter of the year to 187 tonnes. But the world gold price, now around $256 per ounce, is much lower than last year's level of over $300. \par \par \uc2  India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves climbed to nearly $33.5 billion on July 30, around $6 billion more than the year before, with the buildup in recent months helped by dollar inflows from foreign funds investing in Indian stock markets. \par \par \uc2  Those funds invested close to one billion dollars in April-July but turned sellers in the first week of August. \par \par \uc2  A likely hike in U.S. interest rates, the second in as many months could further dampen funds' interest in emerging markets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cooled the market, the sentiment has shifted and the rupee will suffer due to lack of flows," V.Ravikumar, Chief Dealer, ABN Amro said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8902wl7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
