\par\par \b \uc2  Slight India rupee rally seen once Kashmir cools. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 1999\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, June 30 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is unlikely to recover much ground once the Kashmir crisis ends, after losing around 1.4 percent against the dollar since the conflict began in mid-May, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Comments by a Pakistani emissary that he expected India and Pakistan to soon draw up a schedule for the withdrawal of infiltrators from the Indian side of Kashmir failed to affect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  If a diplomatic solution does bear fruit, the rupee will certainly react bullishly, but dealers said the gain would likely be limited to 10 to 15 paise. \par \par \uc2  Technical factors, scarce dollar supplies and the restricted trading environment would muffle the reaction, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Technicals seem to be the dominant resistance factor for the rupee," said V. Ravikumar, chief dealer at ABN AMRO Bank. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen four daily closes above 43.33, which is a key chart point. Even if the rupee firms beyond 43.33, it may not breach 43.20. Overall, it is a dollar bullish trend." \par \par \uc2  The rupee stayed in a steady 43.35/36 range on Wednesday, compared to 42.75 on May 26, when India first launched air strikes against the militants it says are backed by Pakistan. \par \par \uc2  "In markets, it is our experience that anything that goes up does not normally come down fully," said Kalyan Mukherjee, treasury head at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "Besides, there is a perception that a weaker rupee is preferable to boost exports. A fall is desired but it should be smooth," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India has denied market speculation that it is seeking a weaker rupee. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official policy toward the rupee is that the market should determine the trend, so long as trading conditions remain orderly. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's month-long descent has not been one-way, which is one of the reasons why the authorities appeared fairly comfortable with the move, although there has been sporadic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks. \par \par \uc2  One reason why \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets cannot react more strongly is a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, introduced when the rupee hit an all time low of 43.70 per dollar last August, on importers re-booking cancelled import contracts. \par \par \uc2  The Kashmir confrontation prompted several corporates with dollar payables, who had stayed on the sidelines during the rupee's 10-month stable trade, into increasing hedging ratios. \par \par \uc2  But because of the ban on re-booking cancelled import contracts, they will be unable to exit those positions. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see much of importer cancellations in event of peace in Kashmir, since they cannot rebook," Ravikumar said. \par \par \uc2  "Besides dollar supplies are not what they used to be and the rupee's appreciation will not be much," he said. \par \par \uc2  Most exporters had cashed in on the rupee's slide in the past few weeks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Investments by foreign funds in Indian equities, which were $578 million in April and May, slowed to $77 million in June. \par \par \uc2  Overseas investors have also sold close to $35 million worth of investments in Indian debt instruments so far in June. \par \par \uc2  Governor Bimal Jalan said last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was keeping a vigil on the market's reactions to events in Kashmir and would do whatever was necessary to maintain orderly markets. \par \par \uc2  He said the RBI added $2.5 billion to its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in March, April and May but had to sell dollars through the State Bank of India (SBI) in the second week of June to defend the rupee. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6u05bfz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
