\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: India Rupee, Bonds Seen Dn Next Wk; Shrs Steady \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ramya Venugopal \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1999\par 16:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The conflict in Kashmir weighed on India's financial markets this week and will continue to cast its shadow, traders said Friday. \par \par \uc2  The markets had hoped that a U.S. diplomatic mission to Pakistan and India would be a step towards an early settlement of the border dispute between the two countries that started May 26. \par \par \uc2  But with no positive news from Pakistan and reports of northeastern Indian states declaring security alerts Friday, funds and other market participants sold shares ahead of the long weekend. The same sentiment also prompted banks to go long on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee (INR) and bonds are expected to fall next week, as companies are expected to buy dollars and banks will try to offload their bond holdings, traders said. \par \par \uc2  However, shares are seen little changed next week, with market participants reluctant to take on large positions while the situation in Kashmir continues, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  India has been engaged in a border conflict with its neighbor Pakistan after launching air strikes on the border May 26 to flush out what it called Pakistan-backed infiltrators. \par \par \uc2  While events on the border initially didn't affect market sentiment too much, the situation is beginning to take its toll on the market as it's over a month since air strikes were first launched. \par \par \uc2  The rupee slipped steadily against the dollar through the week, propped up only by dollar sales by State Bank of India. The rupee, which began the week at INR43.12 against the dollar, ended at INR43.32 - down 0.46%. \par \par \uc2  A further fall was prevented only by a statement from the Reserve Bank Governor Bimal Jalan Friday, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to the State Bank of India to meet the dollar demand in the market, confirming that State Bank's dollar sales this week were only at its behest. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par           Further Decline In Rupee Seen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, dealers see corporate dollar demand continuing next week and\par only active \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can prevent a\par further fall in the rupee, they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupee is expected to trade between INR43.30 and INR43.40, traders\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indian bonds also saw some selling by market players as banks tried to\par "lighten their positions," a dealer at a private bank said. Though most\par market participants are long of government securities, there is a\par feeling of unease as players are trying to offload positions, dealers\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The only reason for this uncertainty is the border situation," a\par trader said. "Logically there should be buying," he added. Liquidity has\par returned to the market once again, he said, and the rupee's fall in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market has also been smooth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although money was tight earlier this week after tax outflows and a\par bond auction in the previous week, the situation has eased in the past\par couple of days. However, bond prices haven't had a major rally yet.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In contrast with the nervousness in the money market, the mood in the\par stock market is buoyant as foreign institutions continued to buy shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, there is a reluctance to take on huge positions as long as\par the border conflict continues. Also, traders said domestic players have\par begun to offload positions as border tensions are taking a toll on\par sentiment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par India's benchmark stock index, the Sensex, has been very steady,\par ending the week at 4111.77 compared with 4109.89 last week. Traders said\par the index hasn't risen too much because of the Kargil issue. Volume,\par however, has fallen significantly, dealers noted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Next week, the stock market is expected to see lack luster trading,\par dealers said. "Even if the market doesn't fall, it definitely won't\par rise," Vivek Mahajan, vice president, IIT Investrust said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Ramya Venugopal; 91 22 2884211; rvenugopal@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 25-06-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1450GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6p04tf0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
