\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:India Rupee Dn On Border Worries, Oil Cos \b0\par\par\uc2  By S. Anuradha \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1999\par 16:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--Fears of protracted border tensions with Pakistan and dollar buying by oil companies caused the Indian rupee to suffer its largest single-day fall Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Bankers are now anxiously waiting to see if the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had been surprisingly quiet Tuesday, will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee fell 0.4% Tuesday to close at INR43.32. This is still well above the all-time low of the rupee of INR43.70 which it hit on August 1998. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said they won't be surprised if the dollar inched close to the INR43.50 level in the current week. Continued reluctance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupee and bad news on the India-Pakistan border conflict will be enough to push the rupee to that level, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone is wondering what will happen next on the border. The possibility of a protracted war is worrying the market," Ravi Vasantraj, vice president, Mecklai Financial Ltd. said. \par \par \uc2  Besides hurting the economy, bankers said continued border clashes will further force foreign institutional investors to slow down purchases of Indian shares, reducing immediate dollar inflows into the system. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors who had bought shares totaling $405 million in May and $224.6 million in April have become wary in June. They have bought a mere $3.7 million in the first half of June. \par \par \uc2  These dwindling inflows have a direct impact on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves which after rising all through May fell by $99 million to $33.433 billion in the week ended June 4. A treasurer at a leading construction company pointed to this reversal of trends as the key reason for sentiment turning against the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Dlr Buying By Oil Companies Hits Rupee \par \par \uc2  Given that the rise in oil prices and border clashes aren't news, the sudden dramatic fall of the curreny Tuesday certainly took many corporates and banks by surprise. \par \par \uc2  What triggered the fall say brokers was some aggressive buying by the State Bank of India, the country's largest state-owned bank, presumably on behalf of oil companies. \par \par \uc2  Analysts point out that oil prices rose by about 50% in the eight weeks ended May 31, and oil company Indian Oil is probably paying for oil which it bought weeks ago, presumably on suppliers' credit. \par \par \uc2  Neither Indian Oil nor State Bank officials were available to confirm these guesses. \par \par \uc2  "There wasn't any other reason for the rupee to fall this sharply," Punit Bhatia, deputy manager at Credit Agricole Indosuez in Bombay said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said these dollar-remittances are expected to continue for some more days and thereby create pressure on the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Many banks had gone short on the dollar on the expectation the Reserve Bank will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the rupee once it crossed INR43.20. But with Reserve Bank not making any attempts to shore up the rupee, many banks were caught short and had to cover their positions. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said the movement of the rupee Wednesday and thereafter will depend largely on what action Reserve Bank mulls and when it chooses to act. \par \par \uc2  A senior monetary policy official told Dow Jones Tuesday that the fall of the rupee doesn't worry the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Our target isn't a number. We are more concerned whether the market is volatile," he said asking not to be named. \par \par \uc2  In a similar vein, last week the deputy governor of the Reserve Bank was quoted saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't stand in the way if the market wanted to push the rupee lower. \par \par \uc2  Although the Reserve Bank subsequently denied that this statement was intended to push down the rupee, bankers insist the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't mind a gradual depreciation of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "The market may have already tested the RBI's patience too far," said a dealer at a state-owned bank. \par \par \uc2  If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to sell dollars, the rupee could stage a smart recovery. But if it doesn't, there will be continued weakness in the rupee, a dealer at a U.S-based bank said. \par \par \uc2  -By S. Anuradha; 9122-2884211; sanuradha@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 15-06-99 \par \par \uc2  1435GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6f04j3k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
