\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee at risk as Kashmir tension escalates. \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 1999\par 05:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 27 (Reuters) - Flows of foreign money into India will shrivel and the rupee will weaken if the current tension with Pakistan over Kashmir escalates into war, dealers said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee, which fell to an all-time closing low of 43.04 to the dollar on Thursday, was expected to hit 43.20-43.30 on Friday and 43.50 if the war clouds did not dissipate, they said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has weakened 0.67 percent since its close on Tuesday, falling on Wednesday on news India had launched airstrikes against infiltrators in Kashmir and on Thursday as Pakistan shot down two Indian jets. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar looks fairly well bid tomorrow," said Janak Desai, treasurer at Standard Chartered Bank. He said it could hit 43.25 unless the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the RBI, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would act to control volatility. \par \par \uc2  "We should see 43.30 tomorrow," said Sunil Sharma, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade at ANZ Grindlays Bank. "But RBI will come in to dampen any sharp moves so there should not be any undue volatility." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is largely closed and controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has record \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $33.250 billion. \par \par \uc2  Curbs on speculation imposed after the rupee hit a record low of 43.7 last August have resulted in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firming, and the RBI has had to buy dollars in recent months to stem its rise. \par \par \uc2  War fears would hit foreign investor sentiment and foreign inflows would be affected, putting pressure on the rupee, dealers warned. \par \par \uc2  Surging inflows from portfolio investors and direct investments have been a major reason behind the rupee's recent strength and helped the rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar supplies will be affected as foreign fund flows will decelerate," Desai said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the rupee's fall on Thursday was in a thin market. \par \par \uc2  "I think quite a few banks were sitting short and when the news hit the screens, they panicked and looked to cover positions," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  "There was very little corporate interest and we really need to see what corporates do tomorrow. I feel what will happen now is the exporters will start to hold back their remittances while importers may start covering more, so there may be pressure on the rupee." ((Giri Venkatesan, Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv5s07drn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
