\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-India RBI policy must factor in govt fall. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Giri Venkatesan \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 1999\par 18:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, April 18 (Reuters) - The goverment's fall and the daunting prospect of yet another shaky coalition means India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to build in checks to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not slide and interest rates remain stable, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Governor Bimal Jalan is due to present the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary and credit policy for 1999/2000 (April-March) on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Bankers have been low-key in their expectations ahead of the policy, but the government's fall on Saturday and prospects of new elections that could trigger populist spending by any government in power will put an additional burden on the RBI. \par \par \uc2  "We can safely kiss good-bye all promises of controlling government expenditure and fiscal deficit," the treasury head of a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  "Politicians will now be out to woo the electorate...there will be all kind of populist programmes and promises made and I expect spending to be out of control by the year-end." \par \par \uc2  But Jalan is no stranger to piloting India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and money markets through turbulent waters. One week after he took over as RBI governor in 1997, the government led by I.K. Gujral fell and the Asian crisis began to buffet India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  When he presented his first credit policy in April 1998, the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) had been in power for around a month and had yet to get its budget passed. Last year, he also had to contend with pressures on the rupee, following economic sanctions after India's nuclear tests and a credit rating downgrade. \par \par \uc2  Bankers say Jalan's pragmatic policy-making and responses to changing market conditions have been instrumental in ensuring ralative calm in India's markets. \par \par \uc2  Saturday's confidence vote brought down Prime Minister Atal Behari Vajpayee's government by just one vote and with such a slim margin the opposition Congress will find it very difficult to run a stable government. Prospects of fresh federal elections, India's third since 1996, therefore loom large. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said apart from the risk of populist spending worsening the fiscal deficit, the RBI would also have to provide safeguards for the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "RBI has to maintain the rupee's stability," said T.R. Sridharan, chairman and managing director of state-run Canara Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The current political situation could put some pressure on the rupee and the RBI's main concern will be to check this." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stability against the backdrop of a volatile political situation was underscored by others too. \par \par \uc2  "The single most important factor on policy-makers' minds right now must be \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability," the treasury head at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the RBI will maintain the status quo on interest rates as they would not want to give even a slight scope for people to pull the rupee down." he said. \par \par \uc2  In any case, a rate cut did not necessarily translate into a boost to the economy, said A.T. Pannir Selvam, chairman and managing director of state-run Union Bank of India. \par \par \uc2  "We saw last year that cuts in lending rates did not have any impact of credit disbursals by banks. So I do not expect any rate signals," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say the RBI has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks, particularly the State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SBI) , in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to ensure there is no undue volatility in exchange rate movements. \par \par \uc2  "From Tuesday, the fall in the rupee was cushioned by a steady stream of dollar offers from the State Bank of India," former RBI deputy governor R. Janakiraman wrote in the Hindu Business Line daily on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4i02m1r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
