\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee seen weak as ally to quit coalition. \b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 1999\par 15:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, April 12 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to come under pressure as a key partner in India's ruling coalition said its withdrawal of support was inevitable, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to provide a safety net, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been biddish since the political uncertainty began and there will certainly be dollar bids tomorrow (Tuesday)," the chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a private bank said. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee could take a dip tomorrow, but it will not be much, maybe around 20-30 paise (0.20-0.30 rupee) at the most," said P.H. Ramaswamy, country treasurer at Credit Lyonnais. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, Jayaram Jayalalitha, leader of the regional All India Anna Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam (AIADMK) told reporters her party would withdraw support to Prime Minister Atal Behari Vajpayee's coalition government. \par \par \uc2  The AIADMK would also work towards bringing a no-confidence motion against the government, she said. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices could open lower on political worries but dealers said the domestic money market was largely inured to politics and liquidity would be the principal factor in driving price movements. \par \par \uc2  "There could be a drive by speculators to drag it (the rupee) lower. But I don't expect too much as importers are fairly well covered and the RBI will be there. They (RBI) have the market firmly in their grip," Ramaswamy said. \par \par \uc2  Ramaswamy said much of the move had already taken place last week, when the rupee broke from a range around 42.30-42.50 per dollar where it had traded since recovering from a record low of 43.70 last August. \par \par \uc2  The AIADMK's support is crucial to ensure that the minority Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), which leads a motley coalition of 19 parties with support from two more from outside, stays in power. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended Monday at 42.685/695 per dollar, off early lows of 42.71/72, but slightly weaker than the weekend close of 42.67/69. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think anything will happen for the next few days so long as the ruling party maintains it has got the strength for a majority in parliament. The pressure will be there, but the market will watch for the next move," said Satish Nayak, Head of Treasury, Barclays Bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the record \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave it sufficient ammunition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly if required although most expected it to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  passively through state-run banks. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves sttod at an all-time high of $32.535 billion on April 1, compared to $31.586 billion a week earlier. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4c037bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
