\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee ends weaker, shaken by politics. \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 1999\par 13:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, April 6 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended Tuesday sharply weaker as importers rushed to buy dollars, shaken by a deteriorating political situation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended at 42.68/70 per dollar, off an intra-day low of 42.78 but much weaker than its Monday close of 42.43/435. \par \par \uc2  Dollar demand spurted as corporates took fright after the All India Anna Dravida Munnetra Kazhagham (AIADMK), which controls 19 seats in parliament, said on Monday its two ministers would resign from the federal cabinet, traders said. \par \par \uc2  This triggered fears the fragile coalition government could fall, they said. \par \par \uc2  The AIADMK said it would take a decision on cutting off all support to the government later. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said forward premiums were less volatile, reflecting less forward hedging and more inter-bank demand. \par \par \uc2  Earlier the cabinet, led by the Bharatiya Janata Party, decided not to concede the AIADMK's demands which included a change of defence minister and reinstatement of a dismissed naval chief. \par \par \uc2  "I am not ruling out another fall later when the parliament session begins and the actual position is known. But, the reaction to current developments should end at current levels," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultant. \par \par \uc2  The fall was aggravated by dollar purchases by the State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  , the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a short-dollar position in the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A lawmaker, close to the AIADMK, said the southern ally would vote against the government if there was a trust vote, and added to the market's concerns. \par \par \uc2  Forward premiums ended at an annualised 6.22 percent for the two-month dollar against Monday's 5.87. \par \par \uc2  The Bombay stock exchange's benchmark index ended 1.42 percent higher on Tuesday, recovering from a 4.53 percent fall the previous day, and this had led to hopes the rupee would also consolidate in current ranges. ((Vidya Ranganathan, Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4603mfp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
