\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia:  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Counter Oil Prices \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ramya Venugopal \par \uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 07:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Reserve Bank of India is concealing a couple of aces up its sleeve, ready to counter any adverse impact high oil prices may have on the value of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Of all Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Indian rupee is the most vulnerable to higher oil prices, offshore analysts warn. A sustained rise in the price of oil threatens to inflate India's import bill, widen the country's already sizable current account deficit and put additional pressure on the rupee, they reason. \par \par \uc2  The Indian government itself has acknowledged the threat. In its Economic Survey released in February, the government warned that the country's growing appetite for imported petroleum products could cause a serious balance of payments crisis, should prices start to rise. \par \par \uc2  With the price of WTI blend crude oil currently trading at a 12-month high of $16.80, up from its near 13-year low of $10.35 hit in December, offshore analysts may be forgiven for thinking that crisis may be looming. But although more expensive oil imports will undoubtedly aggravate India's record trade gap, it would be wrong to assume this automatically translates into rupee weakness, counter analysts in the onshore market. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India still has two cards to play, they say. For one, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to be prepared to deploy its substantial reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prop up the rupee. In addition, the authorities may also cut a deal with the country's main oil importer - state-run Indian Oil Corp. (P.IOL) - directly providing it with a supply of dollars and bypassing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market altogether. \par \par \uc2  In drawing up contingency plans to cope with the effect of higher oil prices, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is merely being prudent. \par \par \uc2  According to the most recent data, India logged a record $7.79 billion trade deficit in the first 10 months of the current fiscal year, up from $5.58 billion in the same period the previous year. \par \par \uc2  Oil imports in those ten months amounted to $4.91 billion - some 14% of total imports. With official government projections showing that the economy will need to import around 78 million tons of crude oil in fiscal 2001-2002, nearly double the 39.37 million tons imported in 1998-99, India can little afford any hike in the crude price. \par \par \uc2  Given the recent rise in the price of oil, however, "the country's total import bill should rise by $500-600 million in the next fiscal year," says to Sandeep Dhingra, a petroleum analyst with Jardine Fleming Securities. \par \par \uc2  "If industrial activity picks up next year, then we will see an increase in demand for oil, which in turn will push up our import bill," agrees Vasan Shridharan, an economist with Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  An increased demand for imported oil will naturally bring an increased demand for U.S. dollars, putting pressure on the rupee. But market participants say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is watching the rupee closely and is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any abrupt slide. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee is undervalued by 2% against the five-country real effective exchange rate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is said to use. In such a situation, I doubt if it will allow the rupee to depreciate further," says Shridharan. \par \par \uc2  Most market observers feel that if a fall in the value of the rupee is unavoidable, the Reserve Bank will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the first time since August 1998, to engineer a gradual depreciation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not lack the resources, they note: India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit a record high of $31.24 billion for the week ending March 19. \par \par \uc2  Although market watchers expect the rupee to remain steady at around INR42.42 to the U.S. dollar in the near term, they will be watching closely to see if the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries can enforce its recent agreement to cut back crude production. \par \par \uc2  If it can, there may be cause for concern later in the year, according to some. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also cut a special deal with Indian Oil Corp. to ensure that the rising oil import bill does not hit the market directly. \par \par \uc2  Market players suggest that the Reserve Bank may be prepared to enter into a series of cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps contracts with the State Bank of India, which conducts most of the company's crude oil purchase business. Under the arrangement, the State Bank of India would be able to access U.S. dollar funding without entering the market directly, which would avoid putting additional downward pressure on the rupee. \par \par \uc2  - By Ramya Venugopal; 91 22 2884211; rvenugopal@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 31-03-99 \par \par \uc2  0554GMT \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv3v02hoq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
