\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee seen opening steady vs dlr, ranged. \b0\par\par\uc2 301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par 05:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 24 (Reuters) - * Indian rupee expected to open steady to firm against the dollar and trade in a range, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  * The rupee ended Friday at 42.55/58 per dollar against its previous close of 42.80/90. It hit a record low of 43.70 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support measures announced last week will keep rupee buoyant in short term but it will weaken over a longer time frame, they say. \par \par \uc2  * Commerce Minister Ramakrishna Hedge on Saturday said exports were the only permanent remedy to boost the rupee. India's exports in April-June 1998 fell by 7.94 percent to $7.49 billion from $8.13 billion in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  * The highest decision-making body of the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), which leads the ruling coalition, on Saturday said that the government must not devalue to boost exports. \par \par \uc2  * Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through swaps on Friday. On Thursday, RBI hiked banks' cash reserve ratio (CRR) to 11 percent from 10 and its repo rate by 300 basis points to eight percent as part of a package to check volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  * Forward dollar premiums expected to remain steady to firm. Current levels attractive for receiving (selling dollars in swap market) but RBI paying expected to keep premiums from falling too much, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  * Dealers say RBI's paying on Thursday and Friday is an indication it wants premiums to remain firm and prevent a scramble for forward cover. \par \par \uc2  * The annualised premium on six-month dollars ended at 12.06 percent against the previous close of 12.43. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8o01djx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
