\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  RBI  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market - dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2 285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 10:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 20 (Reuters) - The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) continued its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in afternoon trade on Thursday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "RBI paid for September," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted premiums to rise to prevent a rush for forward cover, and in keeping with measures announced earlier. \par \par \uc2  The RBI on Thursday afternoon announced a hike in banks' cash reserve ratio (CRR) to 11 percent from 10 and the repo rate to eight percent from five percent. \par \par \uc2  These were part of a series of measures announced by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the rupee hit a record low of 43.70 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The measures boosted the rupee to 42.70 but trade was volatile with wide two-way quotes, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Short-term interest rates are bound to rise after the repo rate and CRR hike," said a dealer. "So a hike in premiums is in line and probably RBI wants premiums to remain slightly high to prevent a rush of forward bookings." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said forward dollar premiums went up for all months although the rise was sharpest for near month. \par \par \uc2  The annualised premiums for one-month dollars shot up to 18.76 percent from Wednesday's close of 9.83. Premiums on two-, three-and six-month dollars rose to 16.80 percent, 15.45 percent and 12.74 percent respectively from 9.34, 9.23 and 9.08. \par \par \uc2  At 1.58 p.m. (0758 GMT), the rupee was quoted at 42.70/85 per dollar. ((Giri Venkatesan, Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8k0186m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
