\par\par \b \uc2  CMIE sees India 98/99 capital inflows down to $1bn. \b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 1998\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, July 10 (Reuters) - Net foreign capital inflows to India are seen falling to $1.0 billion in 1998/99 from $7.7 billion in the previous year, the Centre for Monitoring the Indian Economy (CMIE) said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The CMIE expected foreign funds to make net sales of $1.0 billion in 1998/99 after making net purchases of $752 million in 1997/98. \par \par \uc2  Foreign direct investment was seen lower at $3.0 billion, down from $3.2 billion the previous year. \par \par \uc2  Deposits by non resident Indians were expected to rise to $1.5 billion, up from $1.14 billion. \par \par \uc2  CMIE foresaw net repayments on external commercial borrowings of $500 million in 1998/99 after inflows of $1.0 billion from this source in 1997/98. \par \par \uc2  Repayments on foreign assistance were seen at $1.0 billion after an inflow of $1.5 billion in the previous year. \par \par \uc2  The CMIE expected zero receipts from global depositary receipt issues of Indian companies abroad, after inflows of $500 million from GDRs last year. \par \par \uc2  Net repayments to the International Monetary Fund were expected to be $500 million, the same as 1997/98. \par \par \uc2  Rupee debt servicing outflows were expected to be smaller at $500 million, down from $800 million in 1997/98. \par \par \uc2  As a result of the fall in inflows India's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were seen falling around $5 billion to $21 billion by March 1999 from $26 billion at the end of March, 1998. \par \par \uc2  The estimate on reserves excluded the impact of any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertaken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the year. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade deficit was expected to rise to $15.8 billion in 1998/99 from $15.0 billion last year. \par \par \uc2  Exports were forecast to rise to $35.7 billion from $34.0 billion and imports were seen rising to $51.5 billion from $48.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  Net invisible earnings were seen little changed, rising to $9.8 billion from $9.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  The current account deficit was expected to widen to $6.0 billion in 1998/99 from $5.3 billion, and as a percentage of gross domestic product (GDP) it was expected to rise to -1.7 percent from -1.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  CMIE also forecast growth in industrial output to slow to 4.5 percent in 1998/99 from 6.6 percent last year. \par \par \uc2  And agricultural ouput was expected to post 0.7 percent growth in 1998/99 after a fall of 3.2 percent in 1997/98. \par \par \uc2  GDP gowth is expected to continue its slower trend, the rate dropping to 4.5-5.0 percent in 1998/99 from 5.0-5.5 percent in 1997/98 after averaging seven percent growth in the previous three years. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7a01b01\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
