\par\par \b \uc2  India Sinha says ready to check market volatility. \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1998\par 14:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI, June 23 (Reuters) - Indian Finance Minister Yashwant Sinha said on Tuesday that the financial authorities were ready to take whatever steps required to calm volatility in the markets. \par \par \uc2  "If it is needed, we will take whatever steps are required to calm down volatility in the markets," Sinha told reporters after a meeting with Communications Minister Sushma Swaraj. \par \par \uc2  "We are not in favour of volatility. I have repeatedly said that we are for orderly conditions and if we feel that undue volatility is taking place, then the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) will take whatever steps are needed," Sinha said. \par \par \uc2  Replying to a question at what level there would be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupee, Sinha said: "It's not a question of at what level. If there is a feeling at a point of time that there is too much volatility, that the market is being disturbed, that will be the point of time when the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  On Monday the minister had said the government would not \par \par \uc2  defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There is no question of defending the rupee... If we were defending the rupee we would not have stopped at $1 billion," Sinha told reporters on Monday in Bombay. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  along with investors taking their money home resulted in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves dropping by just over $1 billion in the first two weeks of June to stand at $27.57 billion on June 12. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has lost some seven percent of its value against the dollar since bearish sentiment hit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after India conducted a series of nuclear tests last month. \par \par \uc2  It ended on Tuesday at a new closing low of 42.90/93 per dollar, but off a record 43.02 hit in stray deals earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  ((New Delhi Newsroom +91-11-301-2024 Fax +91-11-301-4043, \par \par \uc2  delhi.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6n027ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
