\par\par \b \uc2  India call ends higher, bond down on weak rupee. \b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 1998\par 13:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, June 9 (Reuters) - Indian call money rates ended higher on Tuesday on a slight squeeze in funds supply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Call money was traded at 6.5-7.0 percent in closing deals on Tuesday against the previous day's close of 6.0-6.10 percent, they said. \par \par \uc2  Stray deals were reported even up to eight percent. \par \par \uc2  "It (rates) opened around 6.0-6.10 and moved steadily after that. Probably, banks are covering excess amounts as sentiment is building up that money could get scarce later," a private bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Banks had over 50 billion rupees outstanding in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repo agreements, which also put pressure on available liquidity, they said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said nearly 30-40 billion rupees would leave banks early next week as they paid advance tax payments of their corporate clients to the Reserve Bank of India. \par \par \uc2  "There is enough money in the system... Rates should hover around 6.0-7.0 percent, but there could be occasional spurts on technical factors such as money being locked up in repos," a primary dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility had not yet impacted call rates, but they could get affected if the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the rupee. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee clocked a fresh low of 42.26 per dollar on demand from importers and foreign institutional investors (FIIs) paring exposure to Indian stocks and debt markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the bond market bore the brunt of the volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and bond prices were down across the board. \par \par \uc2  The 13.05 percent 2007 bond was traded at 105.60 rupees against the previous close of 105.75 rupees. \par \par \uc2  The 12.69 percent 2002 bond was traded at 103.60 rupees against the previous close of 103.70/75. \par \par \uc2  "There is simply no trading sentiment. There may have been some arbitrage as people sold bonds to reinvest in dollars," a foreign bank dealer said. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6903gjd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
