\par\par \b \uc2  Dubai gold down on India rupee, direct imports. \b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 1998\par 17:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  DUBAI, May 31 (Reuters) - Dubai gold re-exports to India have been checked by a weaker Indian rupee and a move to direct imports, Dubai-based bullion traders said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Imports of U.S. dollar-denominated gold have become more expensive for Indian buyers as the rupee lost ground against the dollar on renewed tension between India and arch-rival Pakistan over nuclear testing. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee was last quoted on Saturday at 41.62/69 to the dollar, compared to a year-on-year 35.70/80 rate. \par \par \uc2  The rupee touched an all-time low of 41.77 this week, prompting verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  and direct dollar sales by the State Bank of India in an attempt to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The rupee situation is squeezing trade, the margins and absolute demand," said one Dubai-based gold trader. \par \par \uc2  India's decision to free up gold imports has also continued to cap trade. \par \par \uc2  Dubai's often lucrative link between European and South African bullion suppliers and Indian consumers has been challenged since last year by direct imports of the yellow metal by Indian banks and agencies. \par \par \uc2  The Indian government in October freed up bullion import rules, authorising three state-run agencies and eight banks to import and sell gold freely in the Indian domestic market. \par \par \uc2  Since then one more nominated agency and bank have been added to the list, raising the number of Indian importers who have turned directly to European banks and bypassed Dubai. \par \par \uc2  Traders at Dubai's market have said Indian demand has ebbed by more than 50 percent year-on-year because of the growing impact of direct imports to India. \par \par \uc2  Dubai customs figures showed that Dubai imported 32.2 tonnes of gold in April, down from a record 61.9 tonnes in January and the 54.4 tonnes recorded in April last year. \par \par \uc2  Figures for May imports, expected to be released by mid-June, are also expected to be below the 52.2 tonnes recorded in May 1997. \par \par \uc2  "The trend for less imports continued this month (May)," said one wholesaler in the Dubai gold trade, pointing to the growing impact of last year's liberalisation. \par \par \uc2  Dubai has been the main feeder market into India, the world's largest gold consumer, with 80 percent of the Gulf Arab emirate's total imports of 660 tonnes last year going to India. \par \par \uc2  Sellers continued to point to forecasts of double-digit gold demand growth in India this year. Gold purchases could also be boosted as a refuge against a falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Other factors dampening short-term trade between Dubai and India were the coming Indian budget and a traditional slowdown in import demand in the June and July monsoons, traders said. \par \par \uc2  India's government is scheduled to unveil its federal budget for 1998/99 (April-March) on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Gold can be affected by the budget's impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by any new laws on duties," said one Dubai trader. \par \par \uc2  Dubai's benchmark ten tola bar (TT) gold bar - 3.746 ounces of 24 carat gold - was trading on Sunday at 4,048-4,050 dirhams ($1,102-1,103) in line with weaker international bullion. \par \par \uc2  Spot bullion was last quoted on Saturday at $292.70/293.20 an ounce, down from the London Friday afternoon fix of $293.60 on the back of speculation of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling. \par \par \uc2  Silver was last quoted on Friday at $5.09/5.12 an ounce, down from a week-on-week $5.28/5.32. ($1 = 3.67 dirhams). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5v044sn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
