\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee ends at record low of 41.72/75 per dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 1998\par 12:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 29 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee notched a new record closing level of 41.72/75 per dollar on Friday on nervousness following Pakistan's retaliatory nuclear tests, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In intra-day trade, the rupee touched an all-time low of 41.77. \par \par \uc2  "The nuclear tests were only the latest in a string of bad news hitting the markets," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the rupee closed at 41.37/40. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said most of the trading was in the inter-bank segment - corporate volumes were marginal which took some of the edge off speculation. \par \par \uc2  "Or else, the rupee would have definitely tested 42 (to a dollar)," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened at 41.60/42.00 but dealers reported only quotes and no deals at those levels. \par \par \uc2  It stabilised in morning trade - holding in the 41.45 to 41.50 range upto noon - supported by verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) and direct dollar sales by the State Bank of India (SBI). \par \par \uc2  The RBI, in a statement, said the Pakistan tests had no financial implications for India and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would monitor the markets to ensure orderly conditions. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's (Thursday's) developments have no financial implications for us. Financial markets are expected to continue to function normally. \par \par \uc2  "RBI will closely monitor market developments, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and take such action as may be necessary to ensure continued orderly conditions," the statement said. \par \par \uc2  The government echoed the RBI's statement - Indian finance minister Yashwant Sinha said on Friday that it was government policy to prevent excessive volatility in the rupee's value. \par \par \uc2  However, a lull in dollar supplies in afternoon trade pushed panic buttons - and the rupee plunged to a historic low of 41.77. \par \par \uc2  But continued dollar sales by the SBI saw it recover, only to weaken again on renewed corporate buying, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee cut losses against the forward dollar also on nominal corporate covering, and increased receiving (forward dollar selling). \par \par \uc2  The six month dollar premium ended at 9.11 percent, higher than the previous close of 8.61, but off its intra-day high of 9.69 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Some corporates are covering positions for the near term, but demand is not much," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Besides, some exporters are receiving as premiums are at attractive levels," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were unsure of the rupee's position on Monday ahead of the budget. \par \par \uc2  "Unless there is some unexpected trigger, trading on Monday should be ranged - volumes will be low as all eyes will be on what the budget offers," a dealer said. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5t046ct\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
