\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee, bonds to draw fire on Pakistan N-tests. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Santosh Menon \par \uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 1998\par 22:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 28 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets were likely to feel the heat from Pakistan's nuclear tests when they opened for trading on Friday, senior bankers said. \par \par \uc2  Pakistan on Thursday conducted a series of five nuclear tests to match those carried out by India two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  "There will be a reaction tomorrow morning. The first hour will be crucial, the rupee will test 42 (to the dollar) levels immediately," P.H. Ramaswamy, country treasurer at Credit Lyonnais told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  There were strong expectations the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  overtly to cool the market if the bears run too fast. \par \par \uc2  Stray quotes for the rupee at 41.90 per dollar, after the market closed at an historic low of 41.37/40, showed some banks were poised to react to Islamabad's nuclear tests, news of which surfaced after market hours here. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's previous record intra-day low was struck on Wednesday at 41.52. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has fallen around four percent since India conducted the first of its own tests on May, sparking international fury and U.S and Japanese sanctions. \par \par \uc2  "This will spoil an already bad sentiment. Every bit of news in the last two weeks has been bad," the chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a leading foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said if the rupee tumbled on Friday, it would pull down bonds with it and prematurely abort a fledgling rally in prices seen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is already nervous. Prices began falling after news of the blasts. If the rupee goes down tomorrow, bond prices will also fall," Ashish Parthasarthy, chief dealer for money markets at HDFC Bank, said. \par \par \uc2  "All eyes are on the rupee-dollar rates. Every other variable including interest rates, will have to adjust based on what happens to it," J. Moses Harding, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  head at IndusInd Bank, said. \par \par \uc2  Indian bonds hit the recovery path late on Wednesday after the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) sent signals it did not view interest rates moving up while fixing the cut-off yield at an auction of nine-year government bonds. \par \par \uc2  Parthasarthy said a rally in any case appeared aborted irrespective of the rupee's behaviour. \par \par \uc2  "Even if the rupee is stable, it will take time for bonds to shed their nervousness," he said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers felt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be forced to directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets if the rupee continued to fall. \par \par \uc2  The RBI had refrained from directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market but had put subtle pressure on the market by checking banks' dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The task of calming the markets by selling dollars was left to the public-sector State Bank of India. But bankers said the RBI may have to step in on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has (verbally) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last few days occassionally. They may step in if the situation warrants it," Martin Fish, chief executive officer of Standard Chartered Bank said. \par \par \uc2  "It may be one of those days when the RBI will have to take things head on," a foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the outlook for the rupee depended on how the corporates reacted. \par \par \uc2  "Corporates will have to keep their cool. Panicky corporates fuel inter-bank speculation. If corporates keep their heads, inter-bank speculation cannot be sustained," Harding said. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, \par \par \uc2  Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5s045ao\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
