\par\par \b \uc2  India confident in face of nuclear sanctions. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Cameron-Moore \par \uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1998\par 20:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 14 (Reuters) - India is confident its economy can weather U.S. sanctions imposed after its nuclear tests this week. \par \par \uc2  "There is absolutely nothing to indicate any loss of confidence among investors either in the stock market, or in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, or in the economy," Finance Minister Yashwant Sinha told reporters late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  India's greatest fear is not of the sanctions themselves, but fear they could precipitate a loss of faith in the country's economy. \par \par \uc2  Following India's nuclear tests the United States said more than $20 billion of aid, loans and trade will be put on ice through sanctions that take effect next month. \par \par \uc2  Japan, India's biggest donor, has order a block on around $1 billion of aid loans and a host of European countries have curtailed aid also. \par \par \uc2  Indian officials say the impact of the U.S. sanctions will be small, as much of the $20 billion is tied to future commitments. \par \par \uc2  And sanctions will hopefully have passed away long before these commitments are due for execution, as all sides scramble to find a diplomatic resolution to the furore. \par \par \uc2  An early Indian signature on the Comprehensive Test Ban Treaty would go a long way to soothing the international community's anger with New Delhi. \par \par \uc2  But for now members of the Hindu revivalist Bhartiya Janata Party led coalition have begun a damage limitation exercise to dispel nagging doubts that sanctions could damage the country. \par \par \uc2  Commerce Minister Ramakrishna Hegde warned Washington's actions would hurt American firms doing business here more than India. \par \par \uc2  And Power Minister Rangarajan Kumaramangalam said India could manage without even multilateral financing for its power projects - the infrastructure sector the government has given the highest priority. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the actual effects on near-term capital inflows will be manageable, officials say. \par \par \uc2  "The direct impact from the U.S. sanctions on capital flows will not be much," said a senior banking official. \par \par \uc2  Doubts on that score finally broke the lock on the rupee on Thursday after it had held steady at below 39.80 since the threat of sanctions first surfaced following Monday's first series of nuclear tests. \par \par \uc2  The rupee closed at 40.51/55, after touching an all time low of 40.77 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  Sinha said the rupee's recovery from lows showed the market mechanism was in place. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, judging conditions fast but not panicky given the extraordinary events. \par \par \uc2  Also the RBI says it has no level in mind for the rupee, and some analysts reckon the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sanguine about letting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken so long as the moves are orderly and justified. \par \par \uc2  But the preservation of confidence appeared to be at play earlier this week when State Bank of India (SBI), which is often regarded as a surrogate for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold dollars to keep the rupee steady after news broke of the nuclear tests. \par \par \uc2  There was also speculation that heavy buying of stocks by the country's largest mutual fund was a veiled attempt by the authorities to keep confidence levels from running too low in financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's close on the benchmark Bombay top 30 index was just three percent down from levels seen before the nuclear storm broke. \par \par \uc2  Fund managers say London's global depository receipt (GDR) market provides a purer gauge of sentiment toward India. \par \par \uc2  The HSBC India GDR index has come down 11 percent in the period, and the GDRs premium over local share prices has fallen to just over seven percent, after having traded at a 15-20 percent premium for the past month. \par \par \uc2  The authorities are monitoring the behaviour of foreign financial institutions (FIIs) in India for signs of trouble. \par \par \uc2  FIIs have invested around $9 billion in India since they first came to the country in 1993. Until last November they built up their investment a little more every month. \par \par \uc2  But in four of the last six months FIIs posted net outflows from India, and the negative trend continued in this month. \par \par \uc2  The scale of the outflow has been small - just $27 million of portfolio investment was pulled out in April, although $32 million left the country in the first week of May. \par \par \uc2  The authorities are not alarmed, and expect the FIIs to top up their investments when the dust has settled, and possibly reap the profits afforded foreigners by a cheaper rupee. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5e0588i\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (india near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or 